### What is the Word Search with a Trie problem?

Given an m x n grid of characters and a list of dictionary words, find all words that can be formed by tracing a path of adjacent cells (up, down, left, right) without reusing any cell in the same path. For example, if the grid contains the letters S, A, L, A, R, Y along a connected path, and `"SALARY"` is in the dictionary, it should be found.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Grid:     Dictionary: ["SALARY", "RENT", "LOAN"]
// S A L
// R E N
// T O A
// "SALARY" is NOT findable (no connected path)
// "RENT" -> R(1,0) E(1,1) N(1,2) T(2,0)? T is not adjacent to N
// Depends on actual adjacency
```

### Why use a trie instead of searching word by word?

Searching for each word separately requires a full DFS per word, which is wasteful because many words share common prefixes. A trie lets you search for all words simultaneously: at each step in the DFS, you follow the trie to check whether any word continues with this prefix. If no word does, you prune immediately. This shared traversal is far more efficient.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Without trie: separate DFS for each of 1000 words -> very slow
// With trie: one shared DFS, each step checks the trie
// If prefix "SAL" has no continuation, prune ALL words starting with "SAL"
// Trie shares prefix work across all words
```

### How do you build the trie?

Insert each dictionary word into the trie character by character. At each terminal node (end of a word), store the complete word string so you can add it to the result without path reconstruction.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TrieNode {
    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];
    String word = null; // stored at terminal nodes
}

public class WordSearchTrie {

    private TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

    private void buildTrie(String[] words) {
        for (String w : words) {
            TrieNode node = root;
            for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {
                int idx = c - 'A';
                if (node.children[idx] == null) {
                    node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();
                }
                node = node.children[idx];
            }
            node.word = w;
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the DFS work with the trie?

From each cell, start a DFS carrying the current trie node. At the cell, check if the trie node has a child for the cell's character. If not, prune: no word continues with this prefix. If yes, descend to that child. If the child is marked as a complete word, add it to the result. Mark the cell visited, recurse into the four adjacent cells, then restore the cell.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class WordSearchWithTrie {

    private TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
    private List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();

    public List<String> findWords(char[][] board, String[] words) {
        buildTrie(words);
        int rows = board.length;
        int cols = board[0].length;

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                dfs(board, r, c, root);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    private void dfs(char[][] board, int r, int c, TrieNode node) {
        if (r < 0 || r >= board.length
                || c < 0 || c >= board[0].length) return;

        char ch = board[r][c];
        if (ch == '#') return; // already visited

        TrieNode child = node.children[ch - 'A'];
        if (child == null) return; // no word continues with this prefix

        if (child.word != null) {
            result.add(child.word);
            child.word = null; // avoid duplicates
        }

        board[r][c] = '#'; // mark visited
        dfs(board, r + 1, c, child);
        dfs(board, r - 1, c, child);
        dfs(board, r, c + 1, child);
        dfs(board, r, c - 1, child);
        board[r][c] = ch; // restore
    }

    private void buildTrie(String[] words) {
        for (String w : words) {
            TrieNode node = root;
            for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) {
                int idx = ch - 'A';
                if (node.children[idx] == null) {
                    node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();
                }
                node = node.children[idx];
            }
            node.word = w;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WordSearchWithTrie solver = new WordSearchWithTrie();
        char[][] board = {
            {'S', 'A', 'L'},
            {'A', 'R', 'Y'},
            {'E', 'N', 'T'}
        };
        String[] words = {"SALARY", "RENT", "SAR", "SALE"};
        System.out.println(solver.findWords(board, words));
    }
}
```

