### Why mark cells with '#' and restore them?

The no-reuse rule means a cell cannot appear twice in the same path. Marking the cell with a sentinel character like `#` during recursion prevents revisiting it. Restoring the original character after recursion allows other paths starting from different cells to use it.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Mark: board[r][c] = '#' prevents re-entering this cell
// Restore: board[r][c] = ch allows other starting paths to use it
// This is the standard backtracking visited-cell technique
```

### How do you avoid duplicate words in the result?

The same word might be found via different paths in the grid. To avoid duplicates, set `child.word = null` after adding it to the result the first time. This ensures each word is only collected once.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// First time finding "SAR":
if (child.word != null) {
    result.add(child.word); // add "SAR"
    child.word = null;       // prevent adding again
}
// Second path to "SAR": child.word is null, skip
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: no words found, single-cell grid, word longer than the grid can accommodate, duplicate words in the dictionary, and words that share prefixes.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: no words match any path -> empty result
// Edge case 2: 1x1 grid with 'A', words=["A"] -> ["A"]
// Edge case 3: word "ABCDEFGHIJ" in a 3x3 grid -> impossible (only 9 cells)
// Edge case 4: words=["SAL","SAL"] -> trie deduplicates, found at most once
// Edge case 5: words=["SAL","SALARY"] -> trie shares prefix, both can be found
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh scans a grid of characters extracted from a cheque image to detect known keywords like SALARY, RENT, and LOAN along connected cell paths, helping classify the cheque's purpose.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class ChequeKeywordScanner {

    public void scanCheque(char[][] grid, String[] keywords) {
        WordSearchWithTrie solver = new WordSearchWithTrie();
        List<String> found = solver.findWords(grid, keywords);
        if (found.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("No keywords detected");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Detected keywords: " + found);
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that trie pruning is an optimization that can be enhanced further by removing trie branches once all words in a subtree have been found (trie pruning optimization). This prevents re-exploring dead subtrees and significantly speeds up dense grids. The worst-case complexity is O(m * n * 4^L) where L is the maximum word length, but trie pruning makes the practical performance much better.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior optimizations:
// 1. Trie branch pruning: remove children once all words found
// 2. Null-out word to avoid duplicates
// 3. Mark cells in-place with '#' instead of using a boolean[][] visited
// 4. Worst case: O(m*n*4^L) but trie pruning makes it much faster
// 5. Store full word at terminal to avoid path reconstruction
```
